title: FirstLoopAaldenburg
tags:
---
<< if DoesHeKnow("SecondLoop") == true >>
	<<jump SecondLoopAaldenburg1>>
<<endif>>

You see a woman before you that stands out. 
Her lab coat, her tidied hair bun, and the notebook she is frantically writing in would all place her in a laboratorium, rather than a parade.
An id-card on her lapel identifies her as “Dr. Aaldenburg - Naturalist Physicist - Nauka Square”. 
She makes no sign of noticing your presence. Her eyes are laser-focused on her notebook, occasionally looking up towards the lake.
<< Learn AaldenburgTarot >>
<< Learn AaldenburgInfoA>>
<< jump FirstLoopAaldenburgTree1 >>
===

title: FirstLoopAaldenburgTree1
---
-> “Hello.” << if $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidHello == false >>
    She does not react. Unless turning to a new page in her notebook is a greeting in her language.
	-> ”Dr. Aaldenburg?”
	    Either she cannot hear you over the sounds of her own writing, or she is too focused on it to pay attention to you.
	    Or she is just ignoring you. Who can say?
        << set $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidHello = true >>
        << jump FirstLoopAaldenburgTree1 >>
-> “Do you know where Dmitriy Yildiz is?” << if $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidDmitriy == false >>
    If she does, she doesn’t tell you. Her writing stays constant, completely uninterrupted by your question.
	-> “Big guy, black hair, was setting things up around here?”
	    No reply. You have a feeling that she hasn’t seen him anyway. She has no eyes for anything besides her notebook, and the lake.
        << set $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidDmitriy = true >>
        << jump FirstLoopAaldenburgTree1 >>
-> “What are you writing in that notebook?” << if $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidHello == true && $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidDmitriy == true >>
    The woman turns towards you for the first time.
    Aaldenburg: “Leave me alone. I am occupied.”
    Then she turns back to her notebook and resumes work.
    You might have to wait until after the parade if you want to know who she is, from the looks of it.
-> “I’ve done a technical study too. Just graduated.” << if $TechMajor == true && $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidHello == true && $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidDmitriy == true >>
    The woman stops writing for a brief moment. She resumes at a faster pace, as if realising she betrayed the fact she did in fact notice you were present.
    Aaldenburg: Come talk to me at Nauka Square then later.
    Aaldenburg: I am engaged in field work at this moment. I cannot chit-chat.
    You have an inkling that she will not elaborate on what that field work consists of at this moment.
    You’ll have to wait until after the parade if you want to know who she is, then.

===

title: SecondLoopAaldenburg1
tags:
---

// plays when first talking to Aaldenburg, when getting the radio quest

There is not an idle moment for the woman in the scorched lab coat you see before you. 
She tinkers with this gizmo, she twiddles with another gadget, dancing through the lab in a practised smooth continuous motion. 
She is robotic, following a program of stimuli and response, a set of rules of how everything in the world should be touched, probed, read, and processed.
<< if $PsychMajor == true >>
    Her mannerisms are hard to parse without getting dizzy. 
    Your psychology know-how might not get you very far with her, though you can definitely tell she must have some sort of neurodevelopmental disorder.
<< elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
    The message she broadcasts is clear: She’s a serious woman of numbers and facts, and she has no care for whimsical nonsense. 
    It’s an ironic contrast with the way she hops between stations, almost like she is skipping. 
    Her half-black, half-red lipstick is quite pretty too.
<< elseif $TechMajor == true >>
	Before you’ve even talked to her, you already feel a rare kinship with her. 
    This is someone who sees the world exactly as it is, just like you: A huge complex machine, full of levers to pull, gears to oil, shiny lights to read and reactions to measure. 
<< endif >>
You stand still just outside the range of her work area, waiting for her to be done and to acknowledge your presence. 
After a few minutes, the flaws in this hypothesis become clear.
<< if $ThisLoopMetWisteria == false >>
    << jump SecondLoopAaldenburgQuit >>
<< endif >>
<< set $ThisLoopMetAaldenburg = true >>

//If the player knows her name from Loop 1
-> “Excuse me, Dr. Aaldenburg?” << if DoesHeKnow("AaldenburgTarot") == true >>
    There is no sign that she has heard you, for a full minute afterwards.
	Eventually however, she slowly comes to a stop, after finishing a few more tasks and clicking her pen for a last time.
	Aaldenburg: What is the matter?
	She raises her eyebrows in surprise once she finally takes a focused look at you.
	Aaldenburg: I have never seen you before. Are you from outside Solster?
-> “Excuse me, Miss?”
    She stops, abruptly, still holding a pen in one hand and a pipette in the other.
	???: Doctor. Not miss.
	Well, at least you got her to stop.
	You can finally take a good look at her. An id-card on her lapel identifies her as “Dr. Aaldenburg - Naturalist Physicist - Nauka Square”.
    << if $TechMajor == true >>
	    Darn. You were hoping for another fellow engineer.
    << endif >>
	She raises her eyebrows in surprise now that she takes a good look at you as well.
    Aaldenburg: I have never seen you before. Are you from outside Solster?
-> “Could you stop for a second?”
    Either she can’t, or she won’t. She continues her endless tinkering with no reaction to your presence. 
    Just when you are about to talk again, she finally comes to a stop, after finishing a few more tasks and clicking her pen for the last time.
    ???: What is the matter?
    You can finally take a good look at her. An id-card on her lapel identifies her as “Dr. Aaldenburg - Naturalist Physicist - Nauka Square”.
    << if $TechMajor == true >>
	    Darn. You were hoping for another fellow engineer.
    << endif >>
    She raises her eyebrows in surprise now that she takes a good look at you as well.
    Aaldenburg: I have never seen you before. Are you from outside Solster?

<<Learn AaldenburgTarot>>
-> “I’m Sasha. I was born here, but I went to college away from here.”
    Aaldenburg: I suspected so. Your accent says Solster, your outfit says student. Graduate now I take it?
	You can’t tell if that outfit comment was meant to be a dig at you or not. Having talked to Wisteria before might have predisposed you to see insults where none were intended.
	Aaldenburg: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?
-> “No, I’m from here. My name is Sasha.”
    Aaldenburg: I can tell from your accent, yes. Yet I have not seen you or heard from you before, and you seem hard to miss in a town this size.
	Is she saying you look strange? You can’t tell if that comment was meant to be a dig at you or not. 
    Having talked to Wisteria before might have predisposed you to see insults where none were intended.
    Aaldenburg: Are you a college student by chance?
        -> “I was. I graduated.”
		She seems pleased with herself for having figured that out.
		Aaldenburg: The hypothesis is proved correct. You do still have that rebellious college student aesthetic. It must have been a recent graduation. 
        << if $ArtMajor == true >>
		    Curious that she is familiar with the ‘college student’ aesthetic. It makes you wonder what she was like back in her own college days. You have a feeling she was quite different to how she is now…
        << endif >>
		Aaldenburg: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?
	-> “How did you guess that?”
		Aaldenburg gives a light chuckle, or something close to it. Nothing about her face smiles at any moment of it.
		Aaldenburg: I did not guess it, I hypothesised it based on the information I could gather.
		Aaldenburg: You look college aged, and your outfit has some of that rebellious aesthetic that students love. It would explain why I had never seen you before, too.
        << if $ArtMajor == true >>
		    Curious that she is familiar with the ‘college student’ aesthetic. It makes you wonder what she was like back in her own college days. You have a feeling she was quite different to how she is now…
        << endif >>
		Aaldenburg: Since you are back at Solster, I can further hypothesise that you are either visiting family, or graduated. If I were to make an actual ‘guess’, I would say graduated.
		Aaldenburg: This town is not a place one comes back to for the holidays. I could not imagine all that travel to come here, for what?
		Her tone is dismissive, seemingly of the idea that anyone would enjoy coming to Solster at all. You wonder if she’s ever met Fabian.
		Aaldenburg: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?
-> “I’m Sasha. I’m pretty much an outsider here.”
    Aaldenburg: Pretty much? That is curiously non-committal language to use to describe your status. 
	She looks at you, up and down. It reminds you of how Wisteria looked at you too, like she is analysing you through a microscope.
	Aaldenburg: Your accent sounds quite Solsterian for an outsider too. Are you a college student by chance?
        -> “I was. I graduated.”
		    She seems pleased with herself for having figured that out.
		    Aaldenburg: The hypothesis is proved correct. You do still have that rebellious college student aesthetic. It must have been a recent graduation. 
		    Curious that she is familiar with the ‘college student’ aesthetic. It makes you wonder what she was like back in her own college days. You have a feeling she was quite different to how she is now…
		    Aaldenburg: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?
	-> “How did you guess that?”
		    Aaldenburg gives a light chuckle, or something close to it. Nothing about her face smiles at any moment of it.
		    Aaldenburg: I did not guess it, I hypothesised it based on the information I could gather.
		    Aaldenburg: You look college aged, and your outfit has some of that rebellious aesthetic that students love. It would explain why I had never seen you before, too.
		    Curious that she is familiar with the ‘college student’ aesthetic. It makes you wonder what she was like back in her own college days. You have a feeling she was quite different to how she is now…
		    Aaldenburg: Since you are back at Solster, I can further hypothesise that you are either visiting family, or graduated. If I were to make an actual ‘guess’, I would say graduated.
		    Aaldenburg: This town is not a place one comes back to for the holidays. I could not imagine all that travel to come here, for what?
		    Her tone is dismissive, seemingly of the idea that anyone would enjoy coming to Solster at all. You wonder if she’s ever met Fabian.
		    Aaldenburg: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?

<< if $PsychMajor == true >>
	-> Psychology.
	    Aaldenburg: A people-person I see. I’m sure Dr. Wisteria will be delighted.
	    She says this in a tone that lacks even the barest note of enthusiasm. Having met Dr. Wisteria yourself, you fully understand why.
	    You feel the instinct to tell her that she was absolutely not delighted to meet you, but Aaldenburg seems like the type of person who doesn’t like being told things they already know.
<< elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
	-> I went to art school.
	    A brief grimace washes over her face. She is equal parts incredulous and disappointed. As an art major, it's a reaction you are used to.
	    Aaldenburg: Really? I took you for more of an intellectual sort. 
        Aaldenburg: Well, at least you're bringing something new to the town. Artistic pursuits are still more stimulating than just staring at the lake, how most people spend their free time here.
        Not a lot of people in Solster have hobbies. The Solsterian work ethic is ruthless. 
        You’d have expected that Aaldenburg would be behind that, seeing how dedicated she seems to her work. Maybe passion is an important factor for her.
<< elseif $TechMajor == true >>
    -> Engineering.
	    Her eyes barely widen, and her mouth open just ever so slightly. She fails to repress a momentary expression of glee over her face, before returning almost mechanically to her regular robotic look.
	    Aaldenburg: It is always pleasant to meet another fellow professional in the field of the practical sciences.
	    Aaldenburg: Perhaps you are still too green to call you  a professional, but I am sure we will get along well.
	    Aaldenburg: My expertise is in Chemistry, but I dabble in various fields. I have little other choice, given the staffing of my laboratory.
	    With that she means that she has nobody else to work with, from the looks of it. There is another lab coat hanging at the entrance, though. 
	    Does she have an assistant, or is it just a spare?
<< endif >>
A nearby machine suddenly emits a beep. Aaldenburg turns her attention to it, turning a dial down and writing a reading from its display on her notebook.
Aaldenburg: I would not mind chatting with you further on another occasion, but I am rather busy at this time, as you can see. Was there a reason you came to see me?

-> “I just traveled back in time a full day after seeing the void destroy Solster.”
        Aaldenburg: No, you did not. Anything else?
	    If she were less busy, she might have been willing to indulge you, as an exercise in science fantasy, but she does not have the time to hear you out it seems.
	    Your radio issues might get her attention better. Maybe you can circle back onto the whole ‘time travel’ issue once you have dealt with that.
        -> “My radio is acting very strange. I thought maybe someone here at Nauka Square would now what is happening to it”
            Aaldenburg: You have a radio? They are quite rare here in Solster. 
	        Aaldenburg: I am not a repairwoman, but you have made me curious now. Show me.
	        You take out the radio from your pocket. Compared to earlier, it has gone mostly silent, but when you put your ear close, you can still hear some static brewing. 
        -> “Wisteria sent me here to get my radio fixed.”
            Aaldenburg gives you a look as if she had bitten an onion the moment you mention Wisteria.
	        Aaldenburg: Oh, of course she is behind this. She does not miss an opportunity to try to disrupt my work on the day of the parade.
	        Aaldenburg: Fine. Show me that radio.
	        You take out the radio from your pocket. Compared to earlier, it has gone mostly silent, but when you put your ear close, you can still hear some static brewing. 
//Circles back to other two options
-> “My radio is acting very strange. I thought maybe someone here at Nauka Square would now what is happening to it”
    Aaldenburg: You have a radio? They are quite rare here in Solster. 
	Aaldenburg: I am not a repairwoman, but you have made me curious now. Show me.
	You take out the radio from your pocket. Compared to earlier, it has gone mostly silent, but when you put your ear close, you can still hear some static brewing. 
-> “Wisteria sent me here to get my radio fixed.”
    Aaldenburg gives you a look as if she had bitten an onion the moment you mention Wisteria.
	Aaldenburg: Oh, of course she is behind this. She does not miss an opportunity to try to disrupt my work on the day of the parade.
	Aaldenburg: Fine. Show me that radio.
	You take out the radio from your pocket. Compared to earlier, it has gone mostly silent, but when you put your ear close, you can still hear some static brewing. 

Aaldenburg: Ah, an Adalard 22. It is a good model, one of the better domestic radios one can find in the market. You have a good eye.
<< if $TechMajor == true >>
    She has a good eye for this as well, for someone whose speciality lies not in machines. Judging by all the contraptions in this lab, she must have a good amount of knowledge of electronics as well.
<< endif >>
Aaldenburg places the radio on her work desk and begins her examination. She looks at it from every angle, touching every button and turning every dial.
In the meantime, you explain to her what you experienced, the sudden static and interference, and the voice mixed within.
Eventually, she takes a screwdriver and opens it up, looking carefully at the mechanisms within without touching them.
After a few minutes, she reassembles your radio and hands it back. Seems she put it back together perfectly, to your relief.
Aaldenburg: There do not appear to be any defects or malfunctions on the radio itself. Thus, there is nothing to fix here.
Aaldenburg: But… I admit I am curious about that interference you encountered. 
Aaldenburg: As I said, there are very few radios in Solster outside of this laboratory. You might have stumbled upon a novel phenomenon that has not been recorded before. This warrants further exploration.
Suddenly, she walks away deeper in her lab. She opens a cabinet and starts digging around for something inside, amongst a mess of wires and machine parts.
Before you can ask for an explanation, she comes back just as quick and puts a small device into your hand. It has a screen, antenna, and some simple buttons and dials.
Aaldenburg: You are going to do some field work. Take this GPS, and go into the town to find some places where the radio interference is present. Understood?

-> “How does this ‘GPS’ work? I have never heard of it.”
    Aaldenburg: It is a fairly new invention, with limited application. I got one for testing from my old university, as Solster is perfect grounds for a trial.
	Aaldenburg: The GPS sends and receives constant signals to and from a receptor here at Nauka Square. 
    Aaldenburg: By measuring the time between signals and their direction, the device can accurately display your exact coordinates within the town as a vector from the receptor.
    Aaldenburg: It will let you note down your position in numbers, much more precise than using a map.
    -> “What do I have to do exactly?”
        Aaldenburg moves over to a map of Solster hanging on a nearby wall. The void lake is on the center, with the whole town built around it.
	    Aaldenburg: We are going to triangulate the source of the radio interference, by finding several locations around Solster where your radio experiences said interference.
	    Aaldenburg: We already know the Star Hospital is one such location. If you can find three more places where your radio reacts to the interference, we should be able to locate where it is coming from.
	    Aaldenburg: Be precise. If your radio starts making that static noise, then move around your current area until you locate the exact position where the static is the strongest.
	    Aaldenburg: Do you understand your task now?
-> “What do I have to do exactly?”
    Aaldenburg moves over to a map of Solster hanging on a nearby wall. The void lake is on the center, with the whole town built around it.
	Aaldenburg: We are going to triangulate the source of the radio interference, by finding several locations around Solster where your radio experiences said interference.
	Aaldenburg: We already know the Star Hospital is one such location. If you can find three more places where your radio reacts to the interference, we should be able to locate where it is coming from.
	Aaldenburg: Be precise. If your radio starts making that static noise, then move around your current area until you locate the exact position where the static is the strongest.
	Aaldenburg: Do you understand your task now?

//Unlocks after “what do I do exactly?”
-> “Any ideas of where I might find this static?”
    Aaldenburg does not answer right away. She ponders, looking around her lab while she thinks.
	Aaldenburg: The first time the static occurred while you were at the Hospital, right?
	Aaldenburg: The hospital does have some machines in it that could have played a factor, but we can’t state that for certain.
	Aaldenburg: My advice would be to stay observant of what is around you once you do find a place where the static is present. Perhaps in similar places, the static will be present too.
	Aaldenburg: As to how you should find your first place with static, that is merely a matter of legwork. 
    Aaldenburg: You could try to investigate areas of Solster that you have not gone to yet or have transited less since you came back. That would be a good course of action. 
        -> “You’re not coming with me?”
            Aaldenburg: Did you forget already when, approximately seven minutes ago, I said I was currently busy?
	        Aaldenburg: I have already given you more of my time than I do most visitors. Now it is your turn to do your part.
	        Aaldenburg: Plus, field work is good for you. It is good practice, builds experience. I imagine they did not send you outside in your study very often, no?
            << if $PsychMajor == true >>
                You did do some practical assignments, but you suspect her standards for what “practical” means are stricter than those of your teachers.
	            It did not involve anywhere as much walking as she probably believes is necessary.
            << elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
                You’ve been to places for your study, though mainly museums and parks for live drawing. Not so much waddling in mud or climbing up trees to find a radio signal.
            << elseif $TechMajor == true >>
                They did not, no. Your practical assignments primarily took place in a workshop, one not too different from Nauka Square.
            << endif >>
-> “You’re not coming with me?”
    Aaldenburg: Did you forget already when, approximately seven minutes ago, I said I was currently busy?
	Aaldenburg: I have already given you more of my time than I do most visitors. Now it is your turn to do your part.
	Aaldenburg: Plus, field work is good for you. It is good practice, builds experience. I imagine they did not send you outside in your study very often, no?
    << if $PsychMajor == true >>
        You did do some practical assignments, but you suspect her standards for what “practical” means are stricter than those of your teachers.
	    It did not involve anywhere as much walking as she probably believes is necessary.
    << elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
        You’ve been to places for your study, though mainly museums and parks for live drawing. Not so much waddling in mud or climbing up trees to find a radio signal.
    << elseif $TechMajor == true >>
        They did not, no. Your practical assignments primarily took place in a workshop, one not too different from Nauka Square.
    << endif >>
	// Loops back, with this option removed
        -> “Any ideas of where I might find this static?”
            Aaldenburg does not answer right away. She ponders, looking around her lab while she thinks.
	        Aaldenburg: The first time the static occurred while you were at the Hospital, right?
	        Aaldenburg: The hospital does have some machines in it that could have played a factor, but we can’t state that for certain.
	        Aaldenburg: My advice would be to stay observant of what is around you once you do find a place where the static is present. Perhaps in similar places, the static will be present too.
	        Aaldenburg: As to how you should find your first place with static, that is merely a matter of legwork. 
            Aaldenburg: You could try to investigate areas of Solster that you have not gone to yet or have transited less since you came back. That would be a good course of action. 

//Unlocks when you have selected at least the “What do I do exactly” option.
-> “Okay, I think I am ready to go out then.”
	Aaldenburg: Good. You can come back once you have found three static locations, other than the hospital.
	Aaldenburg: Be careful with the GPS. If you break it, you will pay for it.
	Her frown seems to say that you’ll pay for it in more than just money.
	Aaldenburg goes back to her workbench and resumes her tinkering, right back where she left off. 
	You take a look at the device in your hand. The screen reads [2.38161, 5.19372]. 
    << if $TechMajor == true >>
        The display is likely the x and y factors of a distance vector between the device and one of the antennas you saw on the roof of the building.
	    Is it connecting to that big, gold antenna you saw? This could be something to ask about later.
    << else>> 
	    You’re glad that you don’t need to understand how this works.
	<< endif >>
	
	For now, it is time to leave this lab, and go out into Solster.
	END GAME FOR NOW
    << Learn $RadioQuestStarted >>
===


title: SecondLoopAaldenburg2
tags:
---

Aaldenburg has not stopped tinkering since you last saw her. Does she ever take a break?
The sound of the door opening gets her attention, and she turns to you to greet you.
Aaldenburg: Sasha, my new assistant returns. Have you completed your task?
You try not to show it, but being called her assistant is quite exciting.
-> “Not yet.”
    Aaldenburg: Then why did you return? You are not giving up already.
	That was not a question but a statement. She will not allow you to quit until you have finished your job.
	Perhaps you should have not been so quick to accept her mission…
	She has already gone back to her own work. Looks like you’ll have to get back to yours.
	//END CONVERSATION
    << jump SecondLoopAaldenburgQuit >>
-> “What did I need to do again?”
    Aaldenburg: Did you not note it down? The most important rule for any researcher to follow is to always note down anything important.
	Besides all the safety-related rules, you assume. Or hope.
	Aaldenburg: Did you not find my assignment to be important enough to write down? I do not like repeating myself, Sasha.
	You did write it down, didn’t you? You should check your notebook.
	Aaldenburg: Just go find three spots on the town where your radio catches the static interference, and note down the location using the GPS.
	As soon as she has said this, she goes back to her own work. Looks like you’ll have to get back to yours too.
     << jump SecondLoopAaldenburgQuit >>
//Only if you do have the three coordinates
-> “I have all three coordinates you wanted.” <<if DoesHeKnow("AllThreeRadios")>>
    Aaldenburg: Excellent! You are a reliable assistant, Sasha. 
	Aaldenburg approaches you with haste, almost tearing the GPS from your hands. She holds out her hand to ask for your notebook however.
	She understands the importance that a good notebook has for a person. You never touch another person’s notebook without their consent.
	-> “Give her your notebook.”
            She takes it in her hands with as much care as she took the GPS, and lays it down on the table. You already set it to the right page.
			<<jump RadioQuestCompleteAal>>
	-> “Read out the coordinates for her.”
            Aaldenburg: Hold on, hold on. Don’t be so fast.
            She moves on to her own notebook, and starts noting down the numbers as you read them out.
            It takes a couple minutes to get through them all. You are unsure if she really needs all these decimals, but better to play it safe and be precise.
			<<jump RadioQuestCompleteAal>>

===
title: RadioQuestCompleteAal
---
<< Learn RadioQuestComplete>>        
Aaldenburg: Interesting… This might confirm my suspicions.
She moves on to the map of Solster hanging on a nearby wall, and starts marking down the coordinates that you gave her.
Wasn’t the reason why she asked you to use the GPS because using a map would be “too imprecise?” Did she just not trust you to use the map correctly?
Aaldenburg: Did you notice anything peculiar in the areas where you found the static?
    -> “Nothing that springs to mind.”
        Aaldenburg: Unfortunate. Not a setback, however. The data is quite clear here.
		She points towards the middle of the map: The lake. The island on the lake, specifically.
    -> “There were electric cables near all of them that seemed to lead towards the lake.”
        Aaldenburg grins briefly. She seems to have been waiting to hear just that.
		Aaldenburg: Just as I thought! Do you know where all the electricity in Solster comes from, Sasha?
		She is already responding to her own question before you can. She points towards the middle of the map: The lake. The island on the lake, specifically.
		Aaldenburg: The island on the lake is the source of all electricity in this town, and undeniably the source of the interference.

Aaldenburg: All the coordinates point towards the source being the island. Being separate from the rest of the town, there must be a radio device there to contact the mainland in case of emergencies.
She moves over to a large book on her desk that was not there the last time you were here. She must have retrieved it while you were gone.
Aaldenburg opens it, and starts looking through, scanning all the pages, before landing on the one she sought.
Aaldenburg: Try a frequency of 99.235 Hertz
-> Tune the radio to 99.235
	It takes some careful turning of the dial to get the frequency down exactly, but as soon as you have it down, the radio comes to live.

???: Help me! I am trapped! Can someone hear me?
-> “I can hear you!”
	???: Oh, thank Star! I thought nobody was going to answer.
	Rayna: I’m Rayna, and I’m trapped in a really weird situation.
	Rayna: What is your name?
-> “Who are you?”
	???: Oh, thank Star! My name is Rayna, and I’m trapped in a really weird situation.
	Rayna: What is your name?
-> “Are you trapped in my radio?”
	Aaldenburg looks at you incredulously, like she is rethinking everything she thought about you.
	???: No, no. Please, this is not time for jokes. I’m trapped somewhere else, but something really weird is going on.
	Rayna: My name is Rayna. Who are you?

//After asking what your name is
-> “It’s Sasha. What is going on with you?”
    Rayna: Okay, Sasha. I don’t know if you can help, but I don’t know who I could even ask.
	Rayna: This will be hard to believe, but I think I might be stuck in a time loop.
	Rayna: I know it sounds absurd, but please I need you to hear me out.
	-> “I…  I think I am in a loop as well.”
            The voice falls silent for a moment. The static plays alone, echoing in the still laboratory.
		    Rayna: By the grace of Star, it looks like I am not alone anymore.
	-> “Did you see the end of the world too, when the parade ended?”
            The voice falls silent for a moment. The static plays alone, echoing in the still laboratory.
		    Rayna: I did. And you… you did too?
		    Rayna: You’re in this loop too, aren’t you?


This is not what you expected. Not what you could ever have expected. But this might be what you were looking for. It’s time for some questions and answers.
<< jump SecondLoopAaldenburg3 >>
===

title: SecondLoopAaldenburg3
tags:
---
-> “How did you experience the loop? I just went to the parade, and then at the end suddenly the void came over the whole town. Then I was back at the train station this morning, when I arrived in town.” << if $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Experience == false >>
    Rayna: I did not really see all that much, to be honest. I was just in my room, and suddenly, the lights went out.
		Rayna: Not suddenly, as in a blackout, but gradually, like the light itself was being devoured.
		Rayna: I just stood there frozen, cowering. I didn’t know what was happening, and I just saw death approaching me.
		Rayna: Then when the darkness finally got me, I was in my bed, with the light of the morning coming through the window and my lamp working perfectly once again.
		Rayna: I wish I understood what happened there any better than you. It was like suddenly, the void turned against us.
        << set $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Experience = true >>
        << jump SecondLoopAaldenburg3 >>
-> “Where are you right now?” << if $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Identity == false >>
    Rayna: I work and live at the lighthouse, at the island on the void lake. I am sort of an on-site mechanic, I deal with electrical stuff. 
    Rayna: So, I stay at the island for several days a week so I can always be ready to fix power outages and other problems.
    Rayna: I was bummed out that I had to stay at the island alone all day while the parade was happening, but now it has gotten even worse.
    Rayna: Now… I am trapped here, waiting for the world to end again, completely alone and helpless…
	Rayna’s voice cuts off. Amongst the static, you can hear crying.
     << set $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Identity = true >>
     << jump SecondLoopAaldenburg3 >>
-> “Why do you call it a 'loop'? Can we really be sure this time travel is going to happen again?” << if $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Time == false >>
    Rayna: Well, I suppose it is just a hunch right now.
	Rayna: For all we know, the parade could come, the town could be destroyed, and that could be the end of everything forever this time.
	Rayna: But I don’t think it will be that helpful to think that way. At least for now we should try going off the expectation that the loop will hold, until we figure out what is causing it.
     << set $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Time = true >>
     << jump SecondLoopAaldenburg3 >>
-> “What should I do now?” << if $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Time == true && $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Identity == true && $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Experience == true >>
    Rayna: I think we should meet, so we can make a real plan together.
	Rayna: How we can do that though, that is a tricky question.
	Rayna: I can’t leave the island at all. I don’t want to, because my post is too important to leave unguarded. A blackout can be so deadly in this town. But I also just couldn’t even if I wanted to.
	Rayna: There is no boat on the island. I realise now how incredibly stupid of an oversight that sounds like, but normally there are Ferrymen constantly at the ready, and there is always at least one on the island at all times.
	Rayna: It just happens that there is one day of the year where there are no ferries to and from the island. 
	The world could not have chosen a worse day to end.
	Rayna: Just… Figure out some way to get here. Please.
	Rayna: I’m sorry to be putting this responsibility on you, but I really need to meet you. I need to see another person again.
	Rayna: I feel like the isolation is going to drive me insane otherwise. If dying and seeing the world die doesn’t do it first.
	Suddenly, the static cuts off. Your radio still seems to be working fine, but it is not receiving anything.
	Aaldenburg: Connection terminated. Total time: Five minutes and seven seconds.
	Aaldenburg takes some final notes on her notebook before closing it and resting it on the table. You had almost forgotten she was there until now.

-> “What happened? Where did she go?”
    Aaldenburg: She just cut the connection. It is not that complicated.
    Aaldenburg: Your radio is still functional, so she must have just stopped broadcasting.
    -> “Do you have any idea of how I can get to the island? Can I swim there, or do I need a boat?”
        Aaldenburg: No, you cannot swim to the island. You will most likely die.
	    It’s not clear if she means you will drown, or if just touching the void water will kill you. Not a chance you want to take either way.
	    Aaldenburg: As for other options, I am not aware of any.
-> “Do you have any idea of how I can get to the island? Can I swim there, or do I need a boat?”
    Aaldenburg: No, you cannot swim to the island. You will most likely die.
	It’s not clear if she means you will drown, or if just touching the void water will kill you. Not a chance you want to take either way.
	Aaldenburg: As for other options, I am not aware of any.
    -> “What happened? Where did she go?”
        Aaldenburg: She just cut the connection. It is not that complicated.
        Aaldenburg: Your radio is still functional, so she must have just stopped broadcasting.

//After both options are chosen:
Having said that, She goes back to work without skipping a beat, like nothing just happened.

-> “That's it? That is all you have to say?”
    Aaldenburg: We set out to discover what was causing the static interference of your radio. We have solved the problem now.
	Aaldenburg: What else were you expecting?
-> “I guess I’ll be leaving now.”
    No reaction. It seems you used up all of her attention span already.

Looks like it will be up to you to figure out your way to the island. Finding out who this Rayna is, and why you both are the only ones affected by this loop is the next step to take.
Though receiving confirmation that you did really experience armageddon is disturbing, hearing someone else who understands what you just lived has brought a renewed conviction to you.
Compartmentalizing the horror of the reality you are currently living, you ready yourself to head out, with your now silent radio at hand.
<<Learn IslandQuest>>

